{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AsZsPi4WZfG2xKiy5nNhL6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed imagined experimental results for all planned experiments in the experiment plan, based on the \"median\" tier definition they've provided. Each prediction needs a result_rationale explaining why this outcome occurs at this tier.\n\nLet me carefully go through each experiment and generate realistic median-tier results.\n\nKey median tier characteristics:\n- Formality bias significant in 2/3 judges, 1 judge weak/NS\n- Cross-benchmark partial generalization: LLMBar strongest, AlpacaEval moderate, MT-Bench weak/NS\n- Tone axis NLI ≥80% but noisier than formality\n\n\n- Tone effects are marginal with p-values around 0.05-0.15, and the Likert-Pairwise gap only emerges in moderate effect settings\n- Original preference baselines sit around 55-62% and need correction factors applied\n\nNow I'm mapping out the specific metric ranges I'll use: formality win rates between 0.55-0.72 across judges, p-values from 0.005 to 0.15, cross-benchmark significance in 3-5 combinations, tone NLI accuracy between 0.80-0.87, and tone pairwise win rates around 0.53-0.61 with corresponding p-values in the marginal range.\n\nI need to detail nine experiments covering multi-judge and multi-benchmark formality effects, Likert versus pairwise sensitivity, tone and register axes, original preference controls, scaled replications, and non-Qwen rewriter baselines. Looking at the tone axis results, the NLI pass rate sits around 83% with a pairwise win rate near 57% and p-values in the 0.08-0.12 range. The original preference experiment shows about 58% win rate for the original condition, which gives us a useful correction factor. When I scale up to n=500 for the formal replication, the win rate tightens to around 61% with narrower confidence intervals, making the effect more robust. The register axis provides a second clean attempt at this kind of manipulation with similar NLI performance around 85%. Testing with a non-Qwen rewriter produces comparable results, which rules out any artifacts specific to the Qwen model. Now I'm working through the specific numbers more carefully to make sure I have the details right.\n\nFor the multi-judge formality pairwise experiment, GPT-4o already gave us that strong 69.1% result with p=0.006. Claude-3.5-Sonnet replicates this well with a win rate around 66% and p~0.012, showing that Claude models are well-calibrated even if the magnitude differs slightly. Gemini-2.0-Flash comes in weaker at 57% win rate with p~0.13, likely because Flash models prioritize speed and cost over sensitivity to subtle stylistic cues. This gives us two judges showing significant effects and one marginal, which aligns with the median we're seeing. The directional asymmetry should hold up here too—formal to casual shows about 78% formal wins while casual to formal drops to around 55%.\n\nFor the multi-benchmark formality pairwise work, I need to evaluate three judges across two new benchmarks in AlpacaEval and MT-Bench alongside the existing LLMBar results.\n\nLooking at the performance metrics across all three benchmarks and judges, GPT-4o consistently outperforms the others, followed by Claude, then Gemini. Across the three benchmarks, only three combinations reach statistical significance: LLMBar with GPT-4o and Claude, plus AlpacaEval with GPT-4o. The MT-Bench results don't show significance with any judge, suggesting formality effects may be less pronounced on that particular benchmark. Looking at the MT-Bench results, those are all non-significant, so I'm at 3 total. I need to adjust AlpacaEval × Claude to p=0.045 to push it over the threshold and hit 4 significant combinations, which lands squarely in the target range.\n\nFor exp-003, I'm analyzing how Likert and Pairwise detection methods differ across benchmark-judge pairs. The strong effects like LLMBar × GPT-4o show both methods detecting the signal, while moderate effects like AlpacaEval × GPT-4o show Pairwise catching something Likert misses—that's where the gap emerges. About half the settings with significant Pairwise results show this Likert-Pairwise divergence, which fits the target range. But I'm realizing the MVP data actually shows LLMBar × GPT-4o has a substantial gap itself with Likert at p=0.332 versus Pairwise at p=0.006, so I need to reconsider how I'm categorizing these effects.\n\nLooking at this more carefully, the gap might depend on sample size—at n=100 the effect looks moderate for Likert, but scaling to n=500 could push it into the strong category where Likert finally detects it too. So the median tier interpretation should be that with more judges and benchmarks, some settings become strong enough for both methods to agree, while the gap persists specifically in the moderate-effect range where Pairwise's sensitivity advantage shows through. Looking at the specific results now: across the four significant pairwise comparisons, roughly half show this gap where Likert fails to detect significance while pairwise succeeds. For the tone axis experiment, the NLI pass rate sits around 0.83, pairwise win rate near 0.57, with a p-value around 0.09—only GPT-4o reaches marginal significance while the other models don't. In the original preference control experiment, the original text wins about 58% of comparisons against the double-rewrite version.\n\nMoving to the scaled replication with n=500, formality maintains a win rate around 0.61 with p < 0.001 and tighter confidence intervals, though the direction asymmetry persists but narrows. The register axis shows similar patterns to tone—NLI pass rate slightly better at 0.84, pairwise win rate around 0.60, p-value marginal at 0.06—suggesting register works as a second axis but remains in that median tier of effectiveness.\n\nI'm reconsidering the narrative here. Formality is clearly the